      "id": "calls[0]/steps[2].reasoning",
      "type": "thinking",
      "kind": "value",
      "label": "Thinking",
      "value": "Key J gives \"TLLA AOL NYLF IVHA HA AOL VSK ZAVUL WPLY\" - that looks like \"MEET AT THE OLD BRIDGE AT THE ...\" wait no. Let me check: \"TLLA AOL NYLF IVHA HA AOL VSK ZAVUL WPLY\". Hmm, \"AOL\" is \"THE\" in Atbash-like? Actually AOL is a common Caesar cipher for \"THE\" with shift -7 (A->T? no). Let me think: AOL -> THE means A->T, O->H, L->E. That's a shift of -7 (A-7=T, O-7=H, L-7=E). So key J (shift +9? wait).\n\nWait, the decrypt function: key J produced \"TLLA AOL NYLF IVHA HA AOL VSK ZAVUL WPLY\". Let me check: ciphertext C with key J. If key J means shift by 9 (A=0, J=9)? C - 9 = T. Yes! C(2) - 9 = -7 = T(19). U(20)-9=11=L. U-9=L. J(9)-9=0=A. So \"CUUJ\" -> \"TLLA\". Yes key J shifts back by 9.\n\nSo key J gives \"TLLA AOL NYLF IVHA HA AOL VSK ZAVUL WPLY\". Hmm, that doesn't look like English. Wait, \"AOL\" = \"THE\"? A->T, O->H, L->E. That's shift -7. But the whole message with key J is shift -9. So AOL is not THE under this key.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Actually \"TLLA AOL NYLF IVHA HA AOL VSK ZAVUL WPLY\" - maybe it's not English. Let me try other keys. The word \"JXU\" appears twice. If JXU = THE, then key K (shift 10) gives J->T, X->H, U->E. But key K gave \"SKKZ ZNK MXKE HUGZ GZ ZNK URJ YZUTK VOKX\" - \"ZNK\" is JXU under K. ZNK = THE? Z->T, N->H, K->E: that's shift -6. But key K is shift -10. So no.\n\nHmm wait, I need to be careful about direction. Let me just try all keys and find which one reads as English. Let me try more keys. Actually, let me think about which key makes \"JXU\" into \"THE\". J->T: +10. X->H: +10 (X+10=H). U->E: +10. So key that shifts by +10 (i.e., decrypt by moving back 10) makes JXU = THE. The key letter for shift 10 is K (A=0, B=1, ..., K=10). But key K gave \"SKKZ ZNK MXKE HUGZ GZ ZNK URJ YZUTK VOKX\" which has ZNK not THE. So the decrypt function must shift differently.\n\nLet me check: key K output \"SKKZ ZNK MXKE HUGZ GZ ZNK URJ YZUTK VOKX\". Cipher \"CUUJ\" -> \"SKKZ\". C->S: C(2) to S(18) is +16 or -10. U(20) to K(10) is -10. So key K shifts back by 10. So decrypt with key K = shift back 10. Then JXU -> ZNK. So JXU under key K is ZNK, not THE.\n\nSo to get JXU = THE, we need shift back by... J->T is +10, so shift forward? Hmm. If decrypt shifts back by n, then ciphertext J (9) - n = T (19) mod 26 => n = -10 mod 26 = 16. So key P (shift 16) would give JXU -> THE. Let me try key P.\n\nActually, let me just try all keys. Let me try a bunch in parallel.",
